\begin{abstract}
    Chamber ensembles often face participation barriers when scores lack parts matching their specific instrumentation. While generative AI models draft arrangements quickly, they introduce structural drift and formatting errors that force musicians into tedious repair tasks. We present HarmonyForge, an interactive co-creative system that separates musical drafting from explanation. A deterministic SATB (Soprano, Alto, Tenor, Bass) Logic Core generates rule-constrained, instrument-mapped MusicXML harmony parts for an uploaded melody, which users then revise in a note-level editor. Hard constraints ban illegal spacing, voice-crossing, and parallel fifths or octaves; a motion heuristic prefers small pitch-to-pitch changes. An on-demand conversational LLM Theory Inspector explains voice-leading rules in plain language and may suggest repairs, but it never writes to the score without user confirmation. The proposed demonstration lets attendees configure custom ensembles, generate deterministic arrangements, override note placements, trigger theory violations on edit, query the pedagogical assistant, and export MusicXML, MIDI, WAV, or PDF. HarmonyForge provides an open sandbox demonstrating how deterministic constraints and glass-box explainability preserve creative ownership.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Participatory music-making in community and educational settings often stalls when written parts do not match the instruments available. While fully probabilistic AI generators draft arrangements or harmonies quickly \cite{Thickstun:2024}, they often produce opaque structures that require tedious manual correction of voice-leading errors (illegal motion between parts, such as parallel fifths) and range errors \cite{Hadjeres:2017, Deng:2024, Wu:2025}. Musicians maintain greater creative agency when systems explicitly separate automated drafting from interactive steering and explanation \cite{Louie:2020, Fu:2025}.

This extended abstract presents HarmonyForge, a co-creative arranger for classical Soprano, Alto, Tenor, and Bass (SATB) ensembles. Formative need-finding interviews with musicians ($N=21$) surfaced two recurring bottlenecks: the manual labor of clef transposition, and players sitting out when no part matched their instrument. We address these challenges by decoupling drafting, direct editing, and pedagogical feedback into three distinct modules: a rule-based SATB Logic Core \cite{Dajda:2021}, an interactive web score editor \cite{Kotvas:2025}, and an LLM-powered Theory Inspector \cite{Zhou:2024}.

