\section{System Architecture}\label{sec:system_architecture}

\subsection{The Logic Core: Rule-Based Scaffolding}\label{subsec:logic_core}
To prevent the structural errors and opaque repair work common in deep learning models like DeepBach~\cite{Hadjeres:2017} or the Anticipatory Music Transformer~\cite{Thickstun:2024}, the Logic Core uses a deterministic, rule-based approach to automated harmonization~\cite{Dajda:2021, Liao:2025}. Instead of relying on probabilistic guessing, the core algorithmically generates arrangements from explicit music-theory rules.

Following common-practice SATB norms~\cite{Gotham:2021}, the engine infers a chord sequence from the uploaded melody and searches four-part voicings within standard vocal ranges. Absolute prohibitions from Aldwell and Schachter~\cite{Aldwell:2011} (range, spacing, voice-crossing, and parallel fifths or octaves) are treated as hard barriers that the search cannot cross. A motion heuristic then prefers small pitch-to-pitch changes, in the spirit of Fuxian stepwise writing~\cite{Fux:1725}, and a greedy-then-backtracking solver returns a voicing sequence that satisfies all active hard rules.

The final step maps this harmonized scaffold to the user's selected instruments (Figure~\ref{fig:logic_core}). By adjusting registers and writing necessary transpositions directly to MusicXML, the Logic Core maps the SATB scaffold onto even unusual instrumental mixtures (e.g., flute, trombone, and violin) as playable, rule-checked individual parts.

\subsection{The Interactive Editor: Tactile Sandbox}\label{subsec:editor_interface}
After generation, musicians refine the arrangement in a note-level editor on a custom web sandbox built on a patched RiffScore canvas~\cite{Kotvas:2025}, facilitating what Fu et al. identify as a co-creative ``collaging and refinement'' stage~\cite{Fu:2025}. Edits sync immediately on the staff (Figure~\ref{fig:tactile_sandbox}), leveraging direct manipulation to foster user ownership and self-efficacy~\cite{Louie:2020}. Users can selectively regenerate measure ranges or accept targeted LLM pitch suggestions, keeping the human creator in control. To resolve cross-platform accessibility, the editor supports exporting completed arrangements as MusicXML, MIDI, WAV, or OpenSheetMusicDisplay-rendered PDFs with clef transpositions.

