\section{Demonstration Use Case}\label{sec:demo}
We will demo HarmonyForge live at our poster station. Attendees collaboratively arrange for an ad-hoc ensemble:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Upload \& Configure}: Load a melody (MusicXML, MIDI, or PDF) and pick an unusual instrument mix (e.g., Flute, Trombone, Violin).
\item \textbf{Generate}: Run the Logic Core to generate playable parts.
\item \textbf{Edit}: In the Sandbox, move a note to intentionally break a rule (such as creating parallel octaves).
\item \textbf{Inspect}: Open the Theory Inspector, read red/blue audit highlights, and ask the Tutor how to fix the conflict.
\item \textbf{Export}: Toggle letter names (and chords) and download PDF, MusicXML, WAV, or MIDI.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}\label{sec:conclusion}
We have presented HarmonyForge, a co-creative symbolic music arrangement system designed to separate deterministic, rule-based drafting from conversational explanations. By combining a constraint-based logic engine with a flexible note-level editor, the system preserves human creative control while automating mechanical notation tasks.

While preliminary testing has refined the constraint engine, upcoming field studies will measure HarmonyForge's pedagogical and creative impact. Future mixed-methods work with classrooms and ensembles will examine whether the Theory Inspector scaffolds foundational learning without compromising user agency. We aim to grow the theory lexicon beyond Western classical SATB so the system can stand at a crossroads, where rule-based arrangement, pedagogical explanation, and diverse musical traditions can meet. Feedback from the ISMIR 2026 late-breaking demo will guide those evaluations and the next version of this explainable sandbox.

